(*
Issue #550: parsePower's right-associative '**'/'pow' recursion had no
depth guard at all -- unlike every other re-entry into expression
parsing, it calls parsePower directly rather than through parseFactor, so
it never bumped ExprDepth. A long enough '**' chain overflowed the real
C++ call stack DURING PARSING ITSELF, confirmed via gdb to be a clean
Parser::parsePower -> Parser::parsePower -> ... recursion.

100,000 terms is the issue's own reproduction: 50,000 still parsed fine
(and was correctly, cleanly rejected by Sema's own separate ExprDepth
guard); 100,000 reliably overflowed the parser's own stack before Sema
ever ran. Checked in verbatim rather than generated at build time,
matching deeply-nested-parentheses-do-not-crash-the-compiler.pas's own
precedent -- lit has no loop-emitting primitive, and the source is
deterministic; nobody should have to look at 100,000 terms to read this
file, so the lit directives come first. '-std=iso10206' is required:
'**' is Extended Pascal only.

A crash surfaces as a real signal (SIGSEGV etc.), not just a nonzero exit
code -- plain `not` (without --crash) already distinguishes the two: it
fails the whole step if the wrapped command crashes via signal, rather
than treating a crash as just another kind of "the inverted command
succeeded". So the step below only passes if the compiler exits via a
controlled, non-crashing diagnostic -- proving this is a real fix, not
just a report that happens to also be a nonzero exit.
*)

(*
RUN: not %plang -std=iso10206 %s -o %t 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
*)

(*
CHECK: nested too deeply
*)

